window.__MYARC_DOCS_INDEX__ = [{"slug": "install", "title": "Install myArc", "area": "Getting started", "summary": "What your Mac needs, what macOS asks on first launch, the welcome tour, and how myArc updates.", "sections": [{"id": "what-you-need", "title": "What you need"}, {"id": "install-the-app", "title": "Install the app"}, {"id": "with-homebrew", "title": "With Homebrew"}, {"id": "from-the-dmg", "title": "From the DMG"}, {"id": "first-launch", "title": "First launch"}, {"id": "moving-from-myarc-nightly", "title": "Moving from myArc Nightly"}, {"id": "the-welcome-tour", "title": "The welcome tour"}, {"id": "1password-with-touch-id", "title": "1Password with Touch ID"}, {"id": "update-to-a-newer-build", "title": "Update to a newer build"}], "body": "myArc ships as an alpha build, made from the latest code and published without release testing. Each build is a disk image (DMG) on the download page . What you need A Mac with Apple silicon. Intel Macs aren't supported. macOS 26 or later. The glass window frame needs macOS 26, and myArc has only been tested on macOS 26.7. Install the app With Homebrew brew install --cask bitbonsai/tap/myarc Homebrew puts myArc.app in Applications and links the myarc CLI onto your PATH . Updates continue through myArc's built-in updater. From the DMG Download the DMG from the download page. Check the file against the SHA-256 checksum listed next to the download button. Run this in Terminal and compare the two values. They must match. If you kept older builds in Downloads, it prints one line per file. shasum -a 256 ~/Downloads/myArc-*.dmg Open the DMG and drag myArc to Applications. First launch Open myArc from Applications. Each build is signed with an Apple Developer ID and notarized, which means Apple has scanned the build. Gatekeeper, the part of macOS that checks downloaded apps, asks once whether to open an app from the internet. Choose Open. macOS may then ask for your login password so myArc can read the key that protects its saved data. Choose Always Allow. Builds are signed with the same Developer ID, so later updates stop asking. Moving from myArc Nightly Earlier builds shipped as myArc Nightly.app with a separate bundle ID, so they won't auto-update to myArc.app . Install myArc.app from the DMG once, then remove myArc Nightly.app from Applications. On first launch, myArc moves your profile from ~/Library/Application Support/myArc Nightly/ to ~/Library/Application Support/myArc/ and migrates your Keychain encryption key automatically. The welcome tour The first time myArc opens, a welcome window plays with sound. Choose Import from Arc\u2026 to bring your Spaces over, as Import from Arc describes, Start fresh to begin without an import, or Skip tour. Three short practice steps follow: open a site from the command bar, pin a tab with Cmd+D, and switch to another Space or make one. When you choose Start fresh or Skip tour, myArc seeds a myArc Basics folder and opens the quick start page, myarc.dev/welcome , before the practice steps begin. It does this once, and skips it after an Arc import. To take the tour again, open Settings, go to Advanced and click Replay welcome tour. 1Password with Touch ID myArc automatically reads Chrome, Arc and Chromium native messaging manifests on macOS, so the 1Password extension can talk to the 1Password desktop app with no terminal setup. To unlock with Touch ID instead of typing your account password, tell 1Password to trust myArc.app once: Open and unlock 1Password on your Mac. Go to Settings > Browser and click Add Browser. Select myArc.app in Applications, then restart myArc. Update to a newer build myArc checks myarc.dev once a day for a newer build and asks before it installs one. To check right away, choose myArc > Check for Updates\u2026. Each build is signed, and myArc refuses an update whose signature doesn't match. myArc gets no separate security updates between builds, so a Chromium fix reaches you with the next build. To update by hand: Quit myArc with Cmd+Q. Download the new DMG and drag myArc to Applications, replacing the old copy. Open it. The new download goes through the first-launch step again. Your Spaces, tabs and settings live in your user folder, outside the app, so replacing the app keeps them. Your data shows where. The GitHub releases page lists every build."}, {"slug": "import-from-arc", "title": "Import from Arc", "area": "Getting started", "summary": "Import your Spaces, folders, pinned tabs and Favorites from Arc's sidebar file or its export, preview what myArc will add, and import again without duplicates.", "sections": [{"id": "import-into-myarc", "title": "Import into myArc"}, {"id": "what-comes-over", "title": "What comes over"}, {"id": "import-again", "title": "Import again"}], "body": "myArc reads one of two Arc files and adds your Spaces and their links to the sidebar. Arc stays untouched, and so does the file. Arc's sidebar file, StorableSidebar.json , keeps your Favorites apart from your pinned tabs, so they arrive as Favorites. Arc writes it on its own, in ~/Library/Application Support/Arc/ . Use this one if Arc is on your Mac. Arc's export is a Markdown file that lists each Space by name, with the links under it. To make one, open Settings in Arc, go to Advanced and choose Export Data. Import into myArc Press Cmd+, to open Settings and go to the Advanced tab, or click Import from Arc\u2026 on the welcome screen. Click Import from Arc\u2026. When Arc's sidebar file is on your Mac, myArc reads it automatically and opens the preview directly. Only when Arc isn't found does myArc show a file picker so you can choose an exported file or sidebar file manually. Read the preview, then click Import. Cancel closes the preview and writes nothing. The preview counts the Profiles, Favorites, Spaces and pinned tabs the import adds and the pinned tabs already in myArc, and shows how each Arc profile maps to a myArc Profile. It also lists links it leaves out and lines it couldn't read. Nothing loads while you import, and each pinned tab opens its page the first time you click it. When the import finishes, a report counts what was added and each kind of thing it left out or changed. What comes over Each Space becomes a Space with the same name, in Arc's order. If a Space with that name already exists, its links go there. Each folder in a Space becomes a folder with the same name, and its links become pinned tabs inside it, in Arc's order. myArc folders don't nest, so in the sidebar file the links of a folder inside a folder go into the outer one. From the sidebar file, Arc's main profile goes into the Profile you're using, and each other Arc profile maps to a myArc Profile with its Arc name (read from Arc's User Data/Local State , or named Arc Profile 1 , Arc Profile 2 and so on if no name is found), creating that Profile if needed. Favorites of Arc's main profile become Favorites of the Profile you're using; other Arc profiles' Favorites are counted in the report. Each tab keeps its custom name and saved page title. From the export, Arc Favorites become pinned tabs at the top of their Space, and every imported Space joins the Profile you're using, because the Markdown export doesn't record Favorites, Profiles or tab titles. Only http and https links come over. Anything else shows up under the links left out. Temporary tabs, archived tabs, themes, history, passwords, cookies, extensions and Boosts don't come over. Spaces and Profiles explains how Spaces and Profiles work in myArc. myArc refuses a sidebar file over 32 MB and an export over 8 MB. Import again Importing the same file a second time adds nothing. myArc reuses Spaces and folders by name and keeps the pinned tabs they already have. Pinned tabs from an import made before myArc had folders move into their folder. If you export from Arc again later, the next import adds only the new Spaces and links. An import never removes anything. If it fails partway, nothing changes, because myArc tries every import on a copy of your workspace before it writes."}, {"slug": "spaces-and-profiles", "title": "Spaces and Profiles", "area": "Your workspace", "summary": "How Spaces, Profiles, pinned tabs, Favorites and temporary tabs fit together in the myArc sidebar.", "sections": [{"id": "spaces", "title": "Spaces"}, {"id": "profiles", "title": "Profiles"}, {"id": "pinned-tabs-and-favorites", "title": "Pinned tabs and Favorites"}, {"id": "folders", "title": "Folders"}, {"id": "temporary-tabs", "title": "Temporary tabs"}, {"id": "not-in-myarc-yet", "title": "Not in myArc yet"}], "body": "myArc keeps Arc's model. A Space sorts your tabs by topic, and a Profile keeps your logins apart. They are separate things, and several Spaces can share one Profile. Spaces A Space is a set of tabs for one part of your life, such as Work, Reading or Travel. Each Space has its own pinned tabs and temporary tabs, and a name, an icon and a color. To create a Space, click + at the bottom of the sidebar. Give it a name, pick an emoji or icon, choose its Profile and pick a theme color. To switch Spaces, click the Space's dot at the bottom of the sidebar. To rename the current Space, click its name at the top of the sidebar. To change a Space's color or icon, right-click its name and choose Edit Theme\u2026 or Change Icon\u2026. To delete a Space, right-click its name and choose Delete Space\u2026. Its tabs move to Archived Tabs in the Library for 30 days. You can't delete your last Space. Profiles A Profile holds your cookies, logins, history and extensions. Spaces that share a Profile share all of that, plus the Profile's Favorites. A Work Profile and a Personal Profile let you stay signed in to two accounts on the same site. Each myArc Profile is a full Chromium Profile, so the separation is the same one Chrome gives you. To make a new Profile, open the Profile menu while you create a Space and choose New Profile, or use Settings > Profiles. Settings > Profiles lists your Profiles and the Spaces that use each one. To create a Profile, click + below the list. Select a Profile to change it: To rename it, type in Name and press Return. To change its icon, click Change\u2026 next to Icon. To set how long its temporary tabs wait before they're archived, pick a time under Archive tabs after. This needs a window of that Profile open. To delete it, click \u2212 below the list, then Delete Profile. This removes its history, logins, cookies and extensions from your Mac. myArc won't delete your last Profile, a Profile that Spaces still use, or a Profile with a window open. Delete the Spaces that use it and close its windows first, then open Settings from another Profile's window. Switching to a Space in another Profile brings in that Profile's window at the same position and size, so it still feels like one window. Pinned tabs and Favorites Pinned tabs and Favorites both appear as favicon tiles at the top of the sidebar, the Favorites first. A pinned tab belongs to one Space and shows only there. A Favorite belongs to the Profile and shows in every Space of that Profile. Right-click a tab in the sidebar to copy its link, mute or unmute audio, open it in Split View, duplicate it, archive it (or archive tabs below it), or open Move to to pin it, add it to Profile Favorites, file it into a folder or move it to another Space. On a Favorite, Move to Space Pins turns it into a Space pin, and Unpin from Space or Remove Favorite takes a tile away. You can also drag a temporary tab up onto the pinned tiles, the Space name or a folder to pin it, or onto a Favorite tile to make it a Favorite. Drag a pinned tab down into the temporary tabs to unpin it. Closing a pinned tab or a Favorite keeps its tile. The page closes, and clicking the tile opens it again. When one has moved away from its saved page, its row shows both names. Click the back arrow on its icon to return to the saved page, or right-click it and choose Edit Pinned Page > Replace Pinned URL with Current to save the new one. Drag Favorite tiles to reorder them. Folders A folder groups pinned tabs inside a Space. To make one, choose New Folder from the + menu at the bottom of the sidebar, right-click the Space name or empty space in the sidebar, or type \"new folder\" in the command bar. Right-click a folder to rename or delete it, and click it to open or close it. To file a tab, right-click it and choose Move to, then pick the folder. Remove from Folder takes it out again. Folders don't nest. Temporary tabs Every other tab is a temporary tab. Temporary tabs sit in a list below the Space name and the New Tab row. They come back after you quit and reopen myArc. When you close a temporary tab, it moves to Archived Tabs in the Library. So does a temporary tab you haven't looked at for 12 hours. Only time with myArc open counts. To wait 24 hours, 7 days or 30 days instead, change Archive tabs after in Settings > Profiles. Open the Library with the archive icon at the bottom left of the sidebar. It also lists your Spaces and recent downloads. Not in myArc yet Folders inside folders don't work yet. Known limitations has the full list."}, {"slug": "peek", "title": "Peek", "area": "Your workspace", "summary": "How links from pinned tabs and Favorites open in a preview over the page, and how to keep, split, reopen or turn off that preview.", "sections": [{"id": "using-the-card", "title": "Using the card"}, {"id": "bringing-a-peek-back", "title": "Bringing a Peek back"}, {"id": "turning-peek-off", "title": "Turning Peek off"}], "body": "Peek keeps your pinned tabs where you left them. When you click a link in a pinned tab or a Favorite that goes to another site, the page opens in a card over the current one instead of replacing it. The card grows out of the link you clicked and shrinks back when you close it. With Reduce Motion turned on in macOS, it appears and goes without the animation. Links to the same site still open in the pinned tab, and links from your temporary tabs open as they always do. Cmd-click a link to open it in a new tab instead. Using the card As in Arc, the page fills the card, and the rest of the page area dims behind it. Three round buttons sit beside the card's top right corner: The X closes the card. Esc, Cmd+W or a click outside the card close it too. The arrows turn the page into a new tab in Today, with its history. Cmd+O does the same. The split button opens the page in a Split View. A copy of your pinned page goes on the left and the Peek page on the right, as one new split in Today, and the pinned tab stays where it was. Bringing a Peek back If you close a Peek by mistake, press Cmd+Shift+T while the same tab is showing. Only the last Peek you closed comes back, and only when you closed it yourself. If you closed a tab after it, Cmd+Shift+T reopens that tab first. Turning Peek off Open Settings with Cmd+, go to Links and turn off Open a Peek window when clicking on links to other sites, the same switch Arc has there. Links from pinned tabs and Favorites to other sites then open in a new tab, and the pinned page stays where it was."}, {"slug": "shortcuts", "title": "Keyboard shortcuts", "area": "Your workspace", "summary": "The shortcuts that work in myArc today, and the Arc shortcuts that are still missing.", "sections": [], "body": "These shortcuts work in the current build. Standard Chromium shortcuts, such as Cmd+W to close a tab and Cmd+R to reload, work as they do in Chrome. Shortcut What it does Compared with Arc Cmd+T Opens the command bar for a new tab Same Cmd+L Opens the command bar on the current address Same Cmd+S Hides or shows the sidebar Same Cmd+D Pins the current tab to its Space, or unpins it if it's pinned. A Favorite stays: remove it from its right-click menu Same Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+8 Switches to the first to eighth Space of the current Profile. Ctrl+9 switches to the last Same Cmd+Option+Left, Cmd+Option+Right Switches to the previous or next Space Same Cmd+Option+Up, Cmd+Option+Down Opens the previous or next row of the sidebar Same Cmd+Shift+C Copies the current address and shows a Copied Current URL message Same Cmd+Shift+T Reopens the last closed tab, or the Peek you just closed Same Cmd+Shift+N Opens a private window Same Ctrl+Cmd+N Opens a blank window Same Cmd+, Opens Settings Same Cmd+Q Quits without asking. Settings > General has a switch to turn on a warning Same key Ctrl+Tab Switches to your last tab. Hold Ctrl and press Tab again to go further back through the five most recent, and add Shift to go the other way Same Ctrl+Shift+= Opens a Split View next to the current tab Same Ctrl+Shift+- Separates a Split View and keeps both tabs Arc closes the current pane instead Ctrl+Shift+1, Ctrl+Shift+2 Moves focus between the two panes of a Split View Same Cmd+O Turns a Peek card into a tab, or moves a Little Arc page into your current Space Same Cmd+Option+N Opens Little Arc, a small window for a quick page, from any app Same Cmd+1 to Cmd+8 open the first eight rows of the sidebar, Favorites first, and Cmd+9 opens the last. In a Little Arc, Cmd+Option+O opens a menu of your Spaces under the Open in button. Pick one to move the page there. The command bar is the box for addresses and searches. Inside it, the arrow keys move through suggestions, Enter opens the selected one and Escape closes the bar. Type an action, such as pin, archive, split or a Space's name, to run it, or type / to list the actions. Press Tab in the empty command bar to switch to Actions mode, which lists only actions, as Arc does. Press Tab again, or Backspace in the empty field, to go back. Cmd+Shift+C opens the element inspector in Chrome. myArc follows Arc and copies the address instead. Ctrl+Option+Cmd+A is a hidden extra that plays the welcome's arrival again, sound included, without restarting the tour. Esc or a click closes it. To take the whole tour again, open Settings, go to Advanced and click Replay welcome tour. You can't change shortcuts yet. The Shortcuts tab in Settings is greyed out until it's built."}, {"slug": "your-data", "title": "Your data", "area": "Your workspace", "summary": "Where myArc keeps your workspace on disk, how to back it up, and what it sends over the network. There is no account.", "sections": [{"id": "where-it-lives", "title": "Where it lives"}, {"id": "back-it-up", "title": "Back it up"}, {"id": "what-goes-over-the-network", "title": "What goes over the network"}, {"id": "agent-control", "title": "Agent control"}], "body": "myArc has no account and nothing to sign in to. Everything it saves stays on your Mac. Where it lives myArc keeps its data in this folder: ~/Library/Application Support/myArc/ Inside it, each Profile has its own folder. The first is called Default , later ones Profile 1 , Profile 2 and so on. A Profile folder holds Chromium's usual data, such as history, cookies and extensions, plus one myArc file, myarc-workspace-v1.json . That file is your workspace, meaning the Spaces of that Profile with their names, icons and colors, your pinned tabs and Favorites, your temporary tabs and Archived Tabs. It's plain JSON you can open in a text editor. When myArc saves the workspace, it writes a new file and swaps it in, so a save that gets interrupted leaves the previous version in place. Saved passwords and cookies stay in Chromium's own stores. Chromium encrypts them with a key it keeps in your macOS Keychain, under the name myArc Safe Storage. Back it up To back up everything, quit myArc and copy the whole myArc folder somewhere safe. Quitting first means nothing is halfway through a save while you copy. To take your Favorites and pinned tabs to another browser, open Settings > Advanced and click Export Pins and Favorites\u2026, or type Export in the command bar. myArc saves a bookmarks file that any browser can import, with Favorites in a Favorites folder and one folder per Space that keeps your sidebar folders. With more than one Profile, each Profile gets its own folder, even when you export from a private window. What goes over the network myArc adds no analytics and no usage tracking. It makes two kinds of request of its own. myArc asks myarc.dev once a day whether a newer build is out. When you install one, it downloads from GitHub. When a pinned tab or Favorite has no icon yet, myArc asks that site for its icon. The request goes only to that site's domain, carries no cookies and never sends the path of the page you saved. No Google icon service or other third party is involved. Every other request comes from Chromium itself. myArc turns off a few of the ones that go to Google: Chromium no longer asks Google's servers for the current time. Domain Reliability reports, which Chromium sends Google when a connection to one of its sites fails, are off. WebRTC text logs, which video calls can upload for debugging, are off by default. Crash report uploads are off too. Crash reports stay on your Mac. After a crash, myArc can open a filled-in GitHub issue, which goes out only if you submit it. Known limitations covers both. Agent control Agents and scripts can't touch myArc until you turn on Allow agents to control myArc in Settings > Advanced. Until then myArc writes nothing to its control folder. CLI and MCP covers how it works."}, {"slug": "ad-blocking", "title": "Ad blocking", "area": "Your workspace", "summary": "How myArc blocks ads and trackers, and how to turn blocking off or on for one site.", "sections": [{"id": "turning-blocking-off-for-one-site", "title": "Turning blocking off for one site"}, {"id": "more-blocker-settings", "title": "More blocker settings"}], "body": "myArc blocks ads and trackers on every site from the first page you open. It does this with uBlock Origin Lite, which comes with myArc and is installed in each Profile. It needs no account and downloads nothing when it starts. Turning blocking off for one site If a site breaks or asks you to allow ads, open its site info: click the \"i\" at the left of the address field, or choose Window > Ad Blocking for This Site\u2026. Turn off Block ads on this site. The page reloads with nothing blocked. Turn the switch back on to block ads there again. The switch only changes the site you're on. Other sites keep blocking, and so do your other Profiles: each Profile keeps its own list of sites. More blocker settings Under the switch, More blocker settings\u2026 opens uBlock Origin Lite's own panel. It has four filtering modes for the site: no filtering, basic, optimal and complete. Optimal is the default. The switch and the panel change the same setting, so off in one shows as no filtering in the other, and on puts the site back at your default mode. To remove the blocker from a Profile, go to the Extensions page and remove uBlock Origin Lite. myArc doesn't put it back."}, {"slug": "cli-and-mcp", "title": "CLI and MCP", "area": "Control", "summary": "Drive myArc from a script or an AI agent with the bundled myarc command and its MCP server. It's off by default, and tab commands reach only the Agent Space.", "sections": [{"id": "turn-it-on", "title": "Turn it on"}, {"id": "install-the-command", "title": "Install the command"}, {"id": "approve-each-tool", "title": "Approve each tool"}, {"id": "short-commands", "title": "Short commands"}, {"id": "exit-codes", "title": "Exit codes"}, {"id": "run-any-command", "title": "Run any command"}, {"id": "use-it-from-an-mcp-client", "title": "Use it from an MCP client"}, {"id": "claude-code-skill", "title": "Claude Code skill"}, {"id": "how-tools-connect", "title": "How tools connect"}], "body": "myArc comes with a command-line tool, myarc , that drives tabs in the browser. The same tool runs an MCP server, so AI agents such as Claude Code can use the browser too. MCP, the Model Context Protocol, is the standard way AI tools discover and call outside tools. Agents work in the Agent Space, a Space myArc keeps for them, with a bot icon. They can't see or touch tabs in your other Spaces. Turn it on Browser control is off by default. Press Cmd+, to open Settings, go to Advanced and turn on Allow agents to control myArc. The switch covers every Profile. Turning it off disconnects every tool at once. Install the command If you installed myArc with Homebrew, myarc is already linked onto your PATH . Otherwise, link it from inside the app: sudo mkdir -p /usr/local/bin sudo ln -sf \"/Applications/myArc.app/Contents/Helpers/myarc\" /usr/local/bin/myarc The link points into the app, so it keeps working after you replace the app with a newer build. Approve each tool The first time a tool connects, a card at the top right of your myArc window names the tool and asks whether it may control your Agent Space. Choose Allow or Don't allow. The command waits up to 60 seconds for your answer. Allow lasts until you quit myArc. While a tool is working, the bot icon at the bottom of the sidebar shows a small blue dot. To take back control, right-click the bot and stop the tool from its menu, or click Stop next to the Agent Space's name. Stop disconnects the tool, and its next command asks you again. Short commands An outline is a text version of the page, one element per line. Anything you can act on carries a reference such as [ref=e4] : form textbox \"Email\" [ref=e1] [required] value=\"\" button \"Send\" [ref=e4] link \"Docs\" [ref=e5] -> /docs.html The short commands act on the current tab and use the element references from the last outline they printed. myarc open \u003curl> # open a tab in the Agent Space and print its outline myarc snapshot [-i] # print the outline; -i keeps only lines you can act on, with context myarc click @e4 # click an element myarc fill @e1 \"text\" # replace a text field's value myarc select @e3 \"Pro\" # pick an option by value or visible text myarc press Enter [@e1] # send Enter, Tab, Escape, an arrow or one character myarc back | forward | reload myarc close # close the current tab myarc tabs # list Agent Space tabs; * marks the current one myarc use t12 # make another tab current Every action prints a status line, what changed and the new outline, so you rarely need snapshot after a step. After a page navigates, references start again at e1 , so always take them from the latest outline. Options that work with every short command: --tab t12 and --rev 1.2 act on another tab or outline revision for one call. --json prints the raw response. Put -- before text that starts with a dash, as in myarc fill @e1 -- \"-5\" . Only http and https pages open. For screenshots, scrolling and waiting for a page to finish loading, use myarc run with page.screenshot , page.scroll and page.wait (or their MCP tools page_screenshot , page_scroll and page_wait ). click also scrolls its target into view first. Exit codes Code Meaning 0 Success 1 The command returned an error, such as stale for an outdated reference 2 The command line was wrong 3 myArc isn't running, or browser control is off 4 myArc refused control, for example because you chose Don't allow Errors go to stderr. On stale , run myarc snapshot and use the new references. On outcome_unknown , the action may or may not have happened, so read the page before you try again. Run any command myarc commands lists every command that works. myarc run sends one of them with JSON parameters: myarc run space.list myarc run space.create '{\"name\":\"Work\"}' myarc run space.rename '{\"space_id\":1,\"name\":\"Home\"}' These commands work today: Spaces: space.list , space.create , space.select , space.rename and space.ensure_agent , which creates the Agent Space if it's missing. Tabs in the Agent Space: tabs.list , tab.open , tab.activate , tab.close , tab.navigate , tab.back , tab.forward and tab.reload . Pages: page.snapshot , page.screenshot , page.click , page.fill , page.select , page.press , page.scroll and page.wait . The Space commands reach all your Spaces in that Profile, so space.rename can rename one of yours. The first command runs in the window you used last. Later commands stay in that window's Profile until you turn control off or quit myArc. Use it from an MCP client myarc mcp starts the MCP server. It has one tool per command, named with an underscore in place of each dot, so space.create is called space_create there. To add it to Claude Code for every project, run: claude mcp add --scope user myarc -- \"/Applications/myArc.app/Contents/Helpers/myarc\" mcp For other MCP clients, use this server entry once myarc is on your PATH : {\"command\": \"myarc\", \"args\": [\"mcp\"]} Claude Code skill The repository has a Claude Code skill that teaches Claude Code to drive myArc through the short commands. Copy the skills/myarc-browser folder into ~/.claude/skills/ . How tools connect While control is on, myArc publishes its connection details in ~/Library/Application Support/myarc/control/ . Only your user account can read that folder. Tools connect over a local socket with a token that changes each time myArc starts. Nothing listens on the network."}, {"slug": "known-limitations", "title": "Known limitations", "area": "Help", "summary": "What myArc Alpha can't do yet, and what to do after a crash or when something breaks.", "sections": [{"id": "safety-and-updates", "title": "Safety and updates"}, {"id": "video-and-media", "title": "Video and media"}, {"id": "what-doesnt-work-yet", "title": "What doesn't work yet"}, {"id": "after-a-crash", "title": "After a crash"}, {"id": "troubleshooting", "title": "Troubleshooting"}, {"id": "macos-wont-open-myarc", "title": "macOS won't open myArc"}, {"id": "macos-keeps-asking-for-your-password", "title": "macOS keeps asking for your password"}, {"id": "an-agent-says-myarc-cant-be-reached", "title": "An agent says myArc can't be reached"}, {"id": "report-a-problem", "title": "Report a problem"}], "body": "myArc is an alpha build on Chromium 158 dev. Expect rough edges, and Chromium security fixes arrive with the next release. Safety and updates Each build is based on a Chromium 158 development revision, before Chromium ships it as a stable release. There are no separate security updates between builds. myArc updates itself from myarc.dev; older myArc Nightly builds need myArc.app installed by hand once. Install myArc covers both. Saved-password and credential guarantees aren't verified yet. Safe Browsing doesn't scan RAR downloads, because the UnRAR license conflicts with GPL-3.0, the license myArc uses. myArc is an independent project with no ties to Arc or The Browser Company. Video and media There is no Widevine, the copy protection that Netflix, Spotify and Disney+ need, so those services won't play. H.264 and AAC are included, so most other web video plays. What doesn't work yet Arc Favorites imported from Arc's Markdown export arrive as pinned tabs in their Space, because the export doesn't mark them. Import Arc's sidebar file instead, as Import from Arc describes. Folders inside folders. Split View holds two tabs at most, and a split can't be pinned or put in a folder. Peek's split button splits a copy of the pinned page, because a split can't hold a pinned tab yet. Cmd+Z doesn't bring back a closed Peek, so use Cmd+Shift+T. Little Arc windows don't come back after a relaunch. Option-Cmd-N works from any app while Links from other apps open in Little Arc is on in Settings > Links, unless another app such as Raycast already uses that key. Boosts and the Mini Player. The Shortcuts tab in Settings. Only the dark appearance has been compared with Arc. Light mode, Reduce Transparency and full-screen layouts haven't been checked. After a crash After a crash, myArc reopens your last session on its own. If the next launch crashes too, it asks before restoring, so a session that keeps crashing can't trap you. Recent tabs and navigation can still be lost after a crash or a force quit. Pinned tabs, Favorites, folders and Spaces are saved to your workspace file, which Your data describes, before the sidebar shows the change, so a force quit right after keeps it. If that file is ever damaged, myArc opens the copy it kept before the last change and leaves the damaged one beside it as .corrupt . A power cut or a full disk hasn't been tested yet. If a single page crashes, reload it with Cmd+R. The rest of the browser keeps running. A crash also brings up a card that asks \"myArc quit unexpectedly. Report it?\" Report\u2026 opens a new GitHub issue in a tab, filled in with the myArc and macOS versions and the top of the crash log macOS wrote, without the lines that identify your Mac. Nothing is sent until you read it and submit it, which needs a GitHub account. Not Now closes the card. It offers each crash log once. Crash report uploads are off. Chromium keeps crash reports on your Mac, in ~/Library/Application Support/myArc/Crashpad/ . They can hold pieces of the pages you had open, so don't attach them to a public issue. Troubleshooting macOS won't open myArc Each new download is notarized, so macOS only asks once whether to open an app downloaded from the internet. Follow the first-launch steps in Install myArc . macOS keeps asking for your password myArc needs it to read the key that protects its saved data. Choose Always Allow. Nightlies before 2026.11.03 used a different signature, so moving from one of those asks once more. An agent says myArc can't be reached The myarc command exits with code 3 when myArc isn't running or browser control is off. Start myArc and turn on Allow agents to control myArc in Settings > Advanced. CLI and MCP has the details. Report a problem Open an issue on GitHub . Include the myArc version, shown at the top of the app menu next to the Apple menu, your macOS version and the steps that cause the problem. Leave out private pages."}, {"slug": "design-system", "title": "Design system", "area": "Brand and design", "summary": "Brand identity, color tokens, typography, layout, components, native glass surfaces and the product decisions behind them.", "sections": [{"id": "product-decisions", "title": "Product decisions"}, {"id": "brand-identity", "title": "Brand identity"}, {"id": "marks-and-lockups", "title": "Marks and lockups"}, {"id": "color", "title": "Color"}, {"id": "brand-palette", "title": "Brand palette"}, {"id": "light-and-dark-tokens", "title": "Light and dark tokens"}, {"id": "typography", "title": "Typography"}, {"id": "type-scale", "title": "Type scale"}, {"id": "reading-rhythm", "title": "Reading rhythm"}, {"id": "layout-and-surfaces", "title": "Layout and surfaces"}, {"id": "native-browser-glass", "title": "Native browser glass"}, {"id": "components", "title": "Components"}, {"id": "iconography", "title": "Iconography"}, {"id": "motion-and-accessibility", "title": "Motion and accessibility"}, {"id": "licensing-and-provenance", "title": "Licensing and provenance"}], "body": "myArc shares one visual language across its website, user docs and native desktop browser shell. It ships on macOS today, with Linux and Windows ahead using the same tokens and OS-native surfaces. Product decisions Every visual choice traces back to a product constraint in docs/PRODUCT.md and AGENTS.md . Yours to keep Workspace state lives in local JSON files with no mandatory account or behavioral tracking. Interfaces stay free of sign-in walls, upsell banners and telemetry prompts. Familiar from day one Carry forward Arc's sidebar, Spaces, Profiles, pinned tabs, Favorites, command bar, Peek, Split View and Little Arc. Spaces group tabs by topic; Profiles isolate cookies, logins, history and extensions, and several Spaces can share one Profile. Restrained site, personal browser The website uses a quiet editorial grid, clear typography and small blue accents. The browser keeps that clarity while letting Spaces carry warmer themes, custom icons and tactile motion. AI by choice Built-in AI is off by default and never required to browse. External agent control over CLI and MCP is a separate opt-in capability scoped to an Agent Space with a visible Stop control. Motion with meaning Animated onboarding is a release requirement. Motion clarifies spatial transitions and honors prefers-reduced-motion and OS reduce-transparency settings from the first frame. Built in the open Code is GPL-3.0 and plans stay public. Every surface separates working behavior from planned work and documents third-party attribution. Brand identity Write the product name as myArc in display copy, headings and prose. Keep filenames, bundle identifiers, URLs and CLI commands lowercase ( myarc ). The primary tagline is myArc, forever yours. Marks and lockups Two ribbon finishes serve different surfaces: the matte mark sits on light backgrounds and navigation bars, while the glass mark pairs with dark navy stages. Keep 12% clear space around the ribbon and preserve its proportions and colors. Matte ribbon mark for light surfaces and site chrome. Glass ribbon mark for dark surfaces and promotional stages. The horizontal lockup pairs a 1rem ribbon mark with the outlined Outfit ExtraBold wordmark. The custom barless A belongs only to the wordmark; headings and body copy use Outfit's standard letters. Header wordmarks sit at 1.75rem ( 1.375rem below 40rem ) with a .5rem gap ( .375rem below 40rem ), and footer wordmarks sit at 1.5rem . Light lockup: ink wordmark (#182432) on ivory (#F5F2EC). Dark lockup: ivory wordmark (#F5F2EC) on dark navy (#0B1420). Color Warm ivory and deep navy anchor the reading surfaces. Brand blue drives interactive focus and primary actions, while the ribbon spectrum supplies secondary accents for Spaces and artwork. Brand palette Ivory #F5F2EC Ink #182432 Midnight #101C2B Sky #10AEFF Blue #034BF4 Violet #4627EE Orchid #A553EC Pink #FF527E Coral #FFAF8E Light and dark tokens The site follows prefers-color-scheme in HTML and CSS with no theme toggle, stored override or script detector. Semantic CSS variables switch surfaces and text while \u003cpicture> elements swap ink and ivory wordmarks. Primary buttons keep the same blue fill and white label in both themes. --page #F5F2EC #0B1420 Page background behind the grain tile --text #182432 #D2D9E1 Primary body copy and header links --quiet #54616D #B1BCC9 Secondary copy, captions and metadata --panel #101C2B #182432 Code blocks and dark container surfaces --link-solid #034BF4 #8FB1FF Hover links, focus rings and active nav --blue-deep #033DD0 #033DD0 Primary button hover and focus fill --heart #B86B73 #B86B73 Footer heart outline and 40 BPM pulse Typography Outfit is the single embedded variable typeface across weights 100 to 900, falling back to Helvetica Neue and sans-serif. The root stays at 100% so browser text-size preferences work, and every size is authored in rem . Type scale Hero display 5.5rem \u00b7 weight 600 \u00b7 line-height 0.98 \u00b7 Steps to 4.5rem, 3.75rem and 2.5rem on smaller viewports Article / docs title 3.5rem \u00b7 weight 600 \u00b7 line-height 1.10 \u00b7 Steps to 2.5rem below 48rem Section heading 2.75rem \u00b7 weight 600 \u00b7 line-height 1.08 \u00b7 2.25rem below 48rem; 2rem inside docs and blog bodies Subheading 1.5rem \u00b7 weight 600 \u00b7 line-height 1.30 \u00b7 Feature titles, status labels and h3 sections Lede / intro 1.25rem \u00b7 weight 400 \u00b7 line-height 1.50 \u00b7 1.375rem in homepage hero; 1.125rem on small screens Body 1.125rem \u00b7 weight 400 \u00b7 line-height 1.55 \u00b7 Default reading copy, capped at 68ch UI / code / footer 1rem \u00b7 weight 400\u2013600 \u00b7 line-height 1.40 \u00b7 Nav links, tables, captions, kbd chips and inline code Reading rhythm Section headings sit above their content and share a single scale per breakpoint. Long-form prose in docs and blog posts caps at 68ch so lines stay easy to scan. Inline code keeps Outfit inside a subtly bordered pill; fenced blocks sit on --panel with --ivory text. Space separates sections rather than horizontal divider rules. Layout and surfaces The site wrapper caps at 80rem with 3rem desktop gutters and 1.5rem mobile gutters at 48rem and below. Sections use 6rem vertical padding on desktop and 4.5rem on mobile. A deterministic monochrome grain tile repeats at 16rem square behind page content. Light mode stacks two layers of the half-opacity WebP tile so texture reads on ivory; dark mode uses a single layer. Native browser glass Modal dialogs, About myArc, updater sheets and agent prompts share one centralized frosted-glass token set (currently implemented for macOS in browser/foundation/glass_mac.h , with the same geometry and opaque fallback contract planned for Linux and Windows). When OS reduce-transparency is on, or on systems without window blur, glass surfaces switch cleanly to opaque tinted fills. Frosted dialog surface 16 DIP outer corners, 1 DIP specular rim, 24 DIP interior padding and 7 DIP action buttons over a blurred backdrop. Allow Cancel kCornerRadius 16 DIP Outer corner radius on all frosted-glass dialogs and modal panels kButtonCornerRadius 7 DIP Corner radius for native dialog action buttons kPadding 24 DIP Standard interior dialog inset across body and actions kTitleTopPadding 36 DIP Top inset clearing the window close control above the title kButtonHeight / MinWidth 36 / 84 DIP Minimum hit target and width for dialog buttons kDarkGlassTint #18181D at 63% Dark frosted tint with a 1 DIP specular rim at 20% alpha kLightGlassTint #FAFAFD at 83% Light frosted tint with a 1 DIP specular rim at 14% alpha GlassMaterial presets 4 levels kWindow, kPopover, kHud and kSheet; opaque fallback under reduce-transparency or without blur Components Interactive elements share the same focus ring ( .1875rem solid var(--link-solid) with .25rem offset) and work without JavaScript. Buttons and links Primary action Secondary action Moving-underline link Pills, status badges and keys Docs Done Partial Missing \u2318L \u2318D \u2303\u21e7= Iconography Browser chrome and site controls use Lucide icons on a 24px grid at stroke width 2 (ISC license): sidebar actions, Peek controls, command bar search, Settings tabs, close buttons and the footer heart. Space icons use 45 filled Ionicons glyphs (MIT license) alongside Unicode 15.1 emoji with English CLDR search annotations. Status indicators pair text labels with distinct border styles so meaning never depends on color alone. Motion and accessibility Intro motion finishes within five seconds. Same-origin site navigation uses native cross-document View Transitions with a one-second blur and fade while keeping the header crisp. The homepage hero ribbon is drawn in WebGL, pauses off screen and falls back to the static image when motion is reduced. Micro feedback 120\u2013200 ms Link underline shift (200ms), button scale 1.02 (180ms), glass dialogs (200ms) Surface transitions 220\u2013350 ms Screenshot peek lift (300ms), lightbox scale 96% to 100% (300ms), onboarding moves Major reveals 600\u20131000 ms Hero rise (900ms), cross-document View Transitions (1s blur/fade), onboarding reveals Easing curve cubic-bezier(.2, .7, .2, 1) Shared deceleration token (--ease) for entrances and surface motion Heartbeat 1.5 s (40 BPM) Footer heart scales to 1.18 on hover or keyboard focus prefers-reduced-motion: reduce disables View Transitions, smooth scrolling, hover scaling, pulse animations and spatial movement while preserving full content and layout. Native frosted dialogs honor OS reduce-transparency settings and fall back to opaque tinted surfaces where window blur is unavailable. Text contrast meets WCAG AA in both light and dark themes, verified across desktop and mobile viewports. Licensing and provenance myArc code is licensed under GPL-3.0 . Outfit is licensed under SIL Open Font License 1.1. Lucide uses the ISC license, and Ionicons uses MIT. Ribbon marks ( myarc-matte.png and myarc-glass.png ) are user-supplied originals whose redistribution and trademark clearance remain open, as recorded in ATTRIBUTION.md . Downloadable brand assets, wallpapers, social cards and the full visual spec live in design/brand and design.md ."}];
(() => {
  const trigger = document.querySelector('.docs-search-btn')
  const dialog = document.querySelector('#docs-search')
  const index = window.__MYARC_DOCS_INDEX__
  if (!trigger || !dialog || !Array.isArray(index) || typeof dialog.showModal !== 'function') return

  const input = dialog.querySelector('.docs-search-input')
  const list = dialog.querySelector('.docs-search-results')
  const empty = dialog.querySelector('.docs-search-empty')
  const prefix = dialog.dataset.prefix || ''
  const reduced = () => matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches

  let frame = 0
  let timer = 0
  let opener = null
  let active = -1
  let shown = []

  const escapeHtml = text => text.replace(/[&<>"']/g, ch => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;', "'": '&#39;' }[ch]))

  const highlight = (text, terms) => {
    const safe = escapeHtml(text)
    if (!terms.length) return safe
    const pattern = new RegExp('(' + terms.map(t => t.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, '\\$&')).join('|') + ')', 'gi')
    return safe.replace(pattern, '<mark>$1</mark>')
  }

  const snippet = (text, terms) => {
    if (!text) return ''
    const lower = text.toLowerCase()
    let pos = -1
    for (const term of terms) {
      const idx = lower.indexOf(term)
      if (idx !== -1 && (pos === -1 || idx < pos)) pos = idx
    }
    if (pos === -1) return text.slice(0, 140) + (text.length > 140 ? '…' : '')
    const start = Math.max(0, pos - 48)
    const end = Math.min(text.length, pos + 112)
    return (start > 0 ? '…' : '') + text.slice(start, end).trim() + (end < text.length ? '…' : '')
  }

  const search = raw => {
    const query = raw.trim().toLowerCase()
    const terms = query ? query.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean) : []
    if (!terms.length) {
      return index.map(doc => ({
        href: prefix + doc.slug + '/',
        title: doc.title,
        meta: doc.area,
        copy: doc.summary,
        terms: []
      }))
    }
    const matches = []
    for (const doc of index) {
      const titleLow = doc.title.toLowerCase()
      const areaLow = doc.area.toLowerCase()
      const summaryLow = doc.summary.toLowerCase()
      const bodyLow = doc.body.toLowerCase()
      const haystack = titleLow + ' ' + areaLow + ' ' + summaryLow + ' ' + bodyLow + ' ' + doc.sections.map(s => s.title.toLowerCase()).join(' ')
      if (!terms.every(term => haystack.includes(term))) continue
      let score = 0
      let bestSection = null
      for (const term of terms) {
        if (titleLow === query) score += 120
        else if (titleLow.startsWith(term)) score += 60
        else if (titleLow.includes(term)) score += 40
        if (areaLow.includes(term)) score += 12
        if (summaryLow.includes(term)) score += 16
        if (bodyLow.includes(term)) score += 8
        for (const section of doc.sections) {
          const secLow = section.title.toLowerCase()
          if (secLow.includes(term)) {
            score += 24
            if (!bestSection) bestSection = section
          }
        }
      }
      matches.push({
        score,
        href: prefix + doc.slug + '/' + (bestSection ? '#' + bestSection.id : ''),
        title: bestSection ? doc.title + ' / ' + bestSection.title : doc.title,
        meta: doc.area,
        copy: snippet(doc.summary + ' ' + doc.body, terms),
        terms
      })
    }
    matches.sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score)
    return matches.slice(0, 12)
  }

  const select = next => {
    const items = [...list.querySelectorAll('a')]
    if (!items.length) {
      active = -1
      return
    }
    active = (next + items.length) % items.length
    items.forEach((item, idx) => item.classList.toggle('is-active', idx === active))
    items[active].scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' })
  }

  const render = () => {
    shown = search(input.value)
    active = shown.length ? 0 : -1
    empty.hidden = shown.length > 0
    list.innerHTML = shown.map((item, idx) => (
      '<li><a href="' + escapeHtml(item.href) + '"' + (idx === 0 ? ' class="is-active"' : '') + '>' +
      '<span class="docs-search-meta">' + escapeHtml(item.meta) + '</span>' +
      '<span class="docs-search-title">' + highlight(item.title, item.terms) + '</span>' +
      '<span class="docs-search-copy">' + highlight(item.copy, item.terms) + '</span>' +
      '</a></li>'
    )).join('')
  }

  const hide = () => {
    cancelAnimationFrame(frame)
    dialog.classList.remove('is-visible')
    clearTimeout(timer)
    if (reduced()) dialog.close()
    else timer = setTimeout(() => dialog.close(), 200)
  }

  const open = () => {
    opener = document.activeElement instanceof HTMLElement ? document.activeElement : trigger
    clearTimeout(timer)
    render()
    if (!dialog.open) dialog.showModal()
    if (reduced()) dialog.classList.add('is-visible')
    else {
      frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
          if (dialog.open) dialog.classList.add('is-visible')
        })
      })
    }
    input.focus()
    input.select()
  }

  trigger.hidden = false
  trigger.addEventListener('click', open)
  input.addEventListener('input', render)
  input.addEventListener('keydown', event => {
    if (event.key === 'ArrowDown') {
      event.preventDefault()
      select(active + 1)
    } else if (event.key === 'ArrowUp') {
      event.preventDefault()
      select(active - 1)
    } else if (event.key === 'Enter' && active >= 0) {
      const current = list.querySelectorAll('a')[active]
      if (current) {
        event.preventDefault()
        current.click()
      }
    }
  })

  list.addEventListener('click', event => {
    const link = event.target.closest('a')
    if (link) hide()
  })

  dialog.addEventListener('cancel', event => {
    event.preventDefault()
    hide()
  })
  dialog.querySelector('form').addEventListener('submit', event => {
    event.preventDefault()
    hide()
  })
  dialog.addEventListener('click', event => {
    if (event.target !== dialog) return
    const box = dialog.getBoundingClientRect()
    if (event.clientX < box.left || event.clientX > box.right || event.clientY < box.top || event.clientY > box.bottom) hide()
  })
  dialog.addEventListener('close', () => {
    cancelAnimationFrame(frame)
    clearTimeout(timer)
    dialog.classList.remove('is-visible')
    if (opener) opener.focus({ preventScroll: true })
  })

  document.addEventListener('keydown', event => {
    if ((event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey) && event.key.toLowerCase() === 'k') {
      event.preventDefault()
      if (dialog.open) hide()
      else open()
    } else if (event.key === '/' && !dialog.open && !event.metaKey && !event.ctrlKey && !event.altKey) {
      const tag = document.activeElement ? document.activeElement.tagName : ''
      if (tag !== 'INPUT' && tag !== 'TEXTAREA' && !document.activeElement?.isContentEditable) {
        event.preventDefault()
        open()
      }
    }
  })
})()
